


HOST(1)             UNIX Programmer's Manual              HOST(1)



NAME
     host - look up host names and addresses

SYNOPSIS
     host name
     host address

DESCRIPTION
     Host looks up Internet host information using the resolver.
     Given a host name, it prints the host's IP address; given a
     dotted-quad IP address, it prints the host's name (reverse
     lookup).

     A name is resolved by first consulting /etc/hosts, then, on
     a miss, by a DNS query to the name server listed in
     /etc/resolv.conf (which dhclient(8) writes from the DHCP
     lease).  An unqualified name (one with no dot) is also tried
     with each domain from the resolv.conf "search"/"domain"
     directive appended, so `host www' resolves as www.example.com
     when the search domain is example.com.  An address is
     resolved by a reverse (PTR) DNS query.

     Output takes one of the forms:

          name has address a.b.c.d
          a.b.c.d domain name pointer name

     If the name or address cannot be resolved, host prints
          Host name not found
     and exits with a non-zero status.

FILES
     /etc/hosts          static host table (consulted first)
     /etc/resolv.conf    name server address

SEE ALSO
     gethostbyname(3), resolv.conf(5), dhclient(8)

BUGS
     Only address (A) and pointer (PTR) records are looked up;
     there is no -t option for other record types yet.  Unlike
     nslookup(8) there is no interactive mode.



